1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,845
Hello, I am Mohammad Alkhatib.

2
00:00:06,125 --> 00:00:14,519
This is my Assignment A3.1, The Creative Plan, for Company 3, Em, Oli & Timmonds: Three Worlds of 2041.

3
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:22,450
It answers one question: how AI could reshape our industry, our customers and our planet over the next 15 years, and what we should build now.

4
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:25,326
Once upon a time.

5
00:00:25,606 --> 00:00:34,766
On 6 April 2026 the Artemis II crew photographed Earth setting behind the Moon, the first crewed lunar flyby since 1972.

6
00:00:35,046 --> 00:00:38,343
I open with it because it shows the impossible moving onto the calendar.

7
00:00:38,623 --> 00:00:46,785
I am the narrator, a consultant at Em, Oli & Timmonds, and the conflict of this story is the uncertainty AI brings to our industry and the whole world.

8
00:00:47,065 --> 00:00:55,018
Over the next slides I take you from today's signals, through our method, into three worlds of 2041, and back to concrete actions for Monday.

9
00:00:55,298 --> 00:00:58,700
The tag at the top of each slide marks our place in the Story Spine.

10
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:00,477
Next comes the map of the journey.

11
00:01:02,377 --> 00:01:03,829
Before the futures, the map.

12
00:01:04,109 --> 00:01:08,509
The question is what E,O&T should build now without betting on one forecast.

13
00:01:08,789 --> 00:01:30,639
I tell the answer as one story with an adapted, branching Story Spine: Every day (signals), But one day (the engine), Because of that three times (three alternative worlds), then two zoom-ins, Until finally (which branch, and which lights to watch), and And ever since then (the business response, a full eight-step story inside one world, the backward plan, the first 90 days and a creative routine).

14
00:01:30,919 --> 00:01:43,457
The scope line keeps the brief concrete: our industry is strategic consulting, our clients are in energy, logistics and digital services, and the world dimension covers work, daily life, infrastructure and space.

15
00:01:45,357 --> 00:01:45,938
Every day.

16
00:01:46,218 --> 00:01:48,772
Before imagining anything, we look at facts.

17
00:01:49,052 --> 00:01:53,986
Harnessing trends, one of the 4 lenses of innovation, gives four measurable signals.

18
00:01:54,266 --> 00:02:03,543
IEA: data centres used about 415 TWh in 2024 and could reach about 945 TWh by 2030.

19
00:02:03,823 --> 00:02:16,617
WEF: 170 million jobs created and 92 million displaced by 2030 across major economic and technological trends, including AI, with 39% of core skills changing.

20
00:02:16,897 --> 00:02:21,796
ESA: about 54,000 debris objects larger than 10 cm in orbit.

21
00:02:22,076 --> 00:02:26,895
NASA: Artemis II flew around the Moon in April 2026.

22
00:02:27,175 --> 00:02:35,801
A second lens, challenging orthodoxies, turns them into one insight: AI behaves like an energy, materials and orbit industry.

23
00:02:36,081 --> 00:02:38,438
These four signals feed every world that follows.

24
00:02:38,718 --> 00:02:41,249
Next: how we turn signals into futures.

25
00:02:43,149 --> 00:02:46,121
But one day we asked: what if these signals keep growing?

26
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:47,805
This is the engine that answers.

27
00:02:48,085 --> 00:03:01,019
Seven creative tools run in sequence: harness trends, counterfactual What if in both directions, a possibility tree with odds, Tick-Tock flips, a moonshot push, hard empathy in five steps, and working backwards.

28
00:03:01,299 --> 00:03:06,047
Combined, they form a more creative kind of scenario planning with contingency built in.

29
00:03:06,327 --> 00:03:12,574
Two critical uncertainties set the axes: how AI is controlled, and whether energy is clean and abundant.

30
00:03:12,854 --> 00:03:17,811
AI variants and exaptation from aviation and game design supplied raw ideas.

31
00:03:18,091 --> 00:03:22,816
Each of the next three slides shows one output of this engine, starting with the best case.

32
00:03:24,716 --> 00:03:27,468
Because of that, world one: Symbiosis.

33
00:03:27,748 --> 00:03:33,634
This is the upper branch of the engine pushed to a moonshot, 10x thinking and a utopia we would want to live in.

34
00:03:33,914 --> 00:03:40,590
Hard empathy is shown in its five steps, in Layla's own voice: scene, feeling, sense-making, worry and action.

35
00:03:40,870 --> 00:03:49,868
Customers meet brands in Mirror Cities, loyalty is designed with white-hat Octalysis drives, transport borrows from air traffic control, and a lunar base becomes real.

36
00:03:50,148 --> 00:03:53,967
For our industry, analysis becomes cheap and judgement becomes the product.

37
00:03:54,247 --> 00:03:59,449
The 5 Cs strip tells the story, and the last line keeps it honest because problems remain.

38
00:03:59,729 --> 00:04:01,784
Next, the same engine run downward.

39
00:04:03,684 --> 00:04:06,261
Because of that, world two: Overheat.

40
00:04:06,541 --> 00:04:12,044
Thinking only upward leads to wishful thinking, so the course pairs every upward What if with a downward one.

41
00:04:12,324 --> 00:04:25,072
The Story Spine chain shows cause and effect: AI demand outruns clean power, pollution and rationing follow, a few players control the models and deepfakes break trust, and a debris cascade knocks out satellites.

42
00:04:25,352 --> 00:04:36,590
Omar walks through the five hard-empathy steps: two conflicting alerts, anxiety, realising one may be synthetic, the worry of an unsafe route, and the action of verifying before dispatch.

43
00:04:36,870 --> 00:04:42,072
The metaverse runs on black-hat Octalysis, and consulting firms without human trust disappear.

44
00:04:42,352 --> 00:04:45,765
Carry-out message: plan for the shadow before it arrives.

45
00:04:46,045 --> 00:04:48,495
Next, the world between these two extremes.

46
00:04:50,395 --> 00:04:54,052
Because of that, world three: Patchwork, the most likely path.

47
00:04:54,332 --> 00:04:57,826
It is built with Tick-Tock: state a fact, then imagine its opposite.

48
00:04:58,106 --> 00:05:04,434
People buying from companies flips to AI agents buying from AI agents, so firms design for machine customers.

49
00:05:04,714 --> 00:05:08,963
Free data flips to owned data, so trust and identity become products.

50
00:05:09,243 --> 00:05:14,468
The world splits into regional blocs with their own AI rules, one planet with three rulebooks.

51
00:05:14,748 --> 00:05:21,365
Sara walks through the five hard-empathy steps and acts by designing one product that passes all three blocs' trust rules.

52
00:05:21,645 --> 00:05:24,977
For E,O&T, the winners translate between blocs.

53
00:05:25,257 --> 00:05:27,428
Next we place all three worlds on one tree.

54
00:05:29,328 --> 00:05:30,037
Zoom in one.

55
00:05:30,317 --> 00:05:34,682
To show how today becomes 2041, I follow one concrete chain.

56
00:05:34,962 --> 00:05:45,724
Drone delivery is already routine: one network passed 2 million commercial deliveries in January 2026, with a median flight of 3 minutes and a delivery every 30 seconds.

57
00:05:46,004 --> 00:05:55,350
A delivery-network model, adapted for orbital logistics, is the bridge: the same kind of autonomy is reaching orbit: robotic freighters berth at the space station today.

58
00:05:55,630 --> 00:06:11,908
Each astronaut needs about 1.7 kg of food a day and fresh produce arrives only about every three months, so in 2041 autonomous cargo drones and orbital farms feed the crew, growing from the first chile peppers harvested on the station in 2021.

59
00:06:12,188 --> 00:06:18,202
This is moonshot thinking, plus exaptation: borrowing a consumer delivery model for space logistics.

60
00:06:18,482 --> 00:06:21,256
Next, a second zoom-in on what becomes valuable.

61
00:06:23,156 --> 00:06:27,742
Zoom in two applies Tick-Tock and challenging orthodoxies to value itself.

62
00:06:28,022 --> 00:06:29,508
Tick: gold is the treasure.

63
00:06:29,788 --> 00:06:32,366
Tock: water, air, sand and plants are.

64
00:06:32,646 --> 00:06:45,045
Water: training GPT-3 was estimated to evaporate about 700,000 litres of fresh water, and 10 to 50 answers could consume about half a litre (Li and colleagues, 2023), while the space station recycles 98% of its water (NASA, 2023).

65
00:06:45,325 --> 00:06:57,144
Sand: 50 billion tonnes a year, about 17 kg per person per day, the most used resource after water (UNEP, 2022); desert sand is too round for concrete and chips start as ultra-pure quartz.

66
00:06:57,424 --> 00:07:04,355
Air: lunar soil is 40 to 45% oxygen by weight (ESA), so oxygen becomes something we mine.

67
00:07:04,635 --> 00:07:09,686
Plants: crops already grow on the station, where fresh food arrives about every three months.

68
00:07:09,966 --> 00:07:13,843
Gold still coats the Webb telescope mirrors, but it no longer measures wealth.

69
00:07:14,123 --> 00:07:25,989
A final What if: if metal-rich asteroids such as 16 Psyche, which NASA's Psyche probe reaches in 2029, were mined and flooded Earth with gold, scarcity would end and so would its value.

70
00:07:26,269 --> 00:07:29,067
Next, all of this goes onto one possibility tree.

71
00:07:30,967 --> 00:07:33,753
Until finally we ask: which world is coming?

72
00:07:34,033 --> 00:07:42,311
Level one gives planning weights to the three worlds: Patchwork 45%, Symbiosis 30%, Overheat 25%.

73
00:07:42,591 --> 00:07:51,159
Level two splits each world on one key driver into three mutually exclusive outcomes with conditional odds that sum to 100% within that world.

74
00:07:51,439 --> 00:08:03,630
Multiplying gives joint odds: for example, Patchwork with partly compatible standards is 45% x 40% = 18%, and all nine joint odds total 100%.

75
00:08:03,910 --> 00:08:08,298
These are illustrative judgements to support discussion, not calibrated forecasts.

76
00:08:08,578 --> 00:08:12,143
Patchwork leads because regulation is already splitting by region.

77
00:08:12,423 --> 00:08:16,881
Next, the lights that tell us which branch is arriving, and what we do when they change.

78
00:08:18,781 --> 00:08:21,416
Until finally, part two: what do we watch?

79
00:08:21,696 --> 00:08:28,627
Possibility trees are only useful with contingency planning, so each signpost has a record, a review trigger and a response.

80
00:08:28,907 --> 00:08:43,397
Clean power for AI points towards Overheat if it worsens; evidence quality separates Symbiosis from Overheat; portability tells us how deep Patchwork goes; orbital access tracks the debris risk; and human agency applies in every world.

81
00:08:43,677 --> 00:08:48,414
The triggers are proposed review rules against a pilot baseline, not external benchmarks.

82
00:08:48,694 --> 00:08:51,120
Next, what E,O&T sells in response.

83
00:08:53,020 --> 00:08:55,470
And ever since then, the business response.

84
00:08:55,750 --> 00:08:59,593
If AI makes reports cheap, E,O&T sells better decisions.

85
00:08:59,873 --> 00:09:12,005
Each world gets a proposed offer: a Future Design Lab for Symbiosis, Verified Decisions and Continuity for Overheat, and an Interoperability Studio for Patchwork, each with a client and a value test.

86
00:09:12,285 --> 00:09:19,681
All three share one asset, the Decision Passport: decision, assumption, evidence, owner, review signal and fallback.

87
00:09:19,961 --> 00:09:23,862
The worked example rehearses Omar's dispatch decision from the Overheat world.

88
00:09:24,142 --> 00:09:27,926
These are proposed concepts, not existing products or results.

89
00:09:29,826 --> 00:09:35,202
The worlds are alternatives, so the story map uses an adapted, branching Story Spine.

90
00:09:35,482 --> 00:09:48,949
Inside a single world, though, a story must be causal, so this slide shows all eight steps for Omar in Overheat: once upon a time, every day, but one day, three because-of-that steps, until finally, and ever since then.

91
00:09:49,229 --> 00:10:02,685
It also carries the 5 Cs: Omar is the character, unreliable dispatch the conflict, verification and fallback the cure, a continued service the change, and the carry-out is that the cure is a process, not perfect AI.

92
00:10:02,965 --> 00:10:04,312
Next, the backward plan.

93
00:10:06,212 --> 00:10:08,430
And ever since then, we plan backwards.

94
00:10:08,710 --> 00:10:15,409
Bent Flyvbjerg works right to left from a sharp picture of the goal, and Amazon writes the press release before building the product.

95
00:10:15,689 --> 00:10:18,754
Our 2041 press release works in any world.

96
00:10:19,034 --> 00:10:34,893
From there: machine-customer strategies by 2035, energy-aware AI and reskilling by 2030, and no-regret moves in 2026 such as a digital-twin pilot, a signpost dashboard and a quarterly day-in-2041 workshop.

97
00:10:35,173 --> 00:10:39,144
The commonplace book and stimuli queue keep the team's slow hunches growing.

98
00:10:39,424 --> 00:10:41,792
Next, the one message to carry out of the room.

99
00:10:43,692 --> 00:10:45,886
And ever since then, the first test.

100
00:10:46,166 --> 00:11:02,525
Working backwards ends with a concrete first step, so the plan for the first 90 days uses one client, one workflow and a limited rehearsal with clear owners and gates: define and observe, prototype and rehearse across all three worlds, then stress-test with ten scripted cases.

101
00:11:02,805 --> 00:11:11,466
The decision at the end is continue, change or stop, and the pilot limit is stated honestly: five users and ten cases support learning, not proof.

102
00:11:11,746 --> 00:11:14,846
Next, the creative routine that keeps the work alive.

103
00:11:16,746 --> 00:11:19,242
And ever since then, the team keeps creating.

104
00:11:19,522 --> 00:11:37,935
A proposed 45-minute monthly workshop puts three course ideas to work: psychological safety in the generate step, white-hat Octalysis drives in the rehearsal (progress, feedback and retries, with no public ranking or penalties), and AI and science fiction as stimuli in the reflect step, captured in a commonplace book.

105
00:11:38,215 --> 00:11:41,025
A monthly stimuli queue keeps feeding slow hunches.

106
00:11:41,305 --> 00:11:42,814
Next, the carry-out message.

107
00:11:44,714 --> 00:11:47,709
The carry-out message closes the loop that opened with the Moon.

108
00:11:47,989 --> 00:11:53,574
Three worlds, three responses: build Symbiosis, shape Patchwork, insure against Overheat.

109
00:11:53,854 --> 00:11:58,103
We do not predict the future; we prepare for all three and work for the first one.

110
00:11:58,383 --> 00:12:03,492
Next, in Part 2, I reflect on which of these tools changed how I work and how I will keep using them.

111
00:12:05,392 --> 00:12:07,354
Part 2, my personal reflection.

112
00:12:07,634 --> 00:12:08,748
What I am taking with me.

113
00:12:09,028 --> 00:12:16,296
Three ideas from this course changed how I think about planning: possibility trees, working backwards, and counterfactual thinking.

114
00:12:16,576 --> 00:12:23,229
Together they gave me a structured language for something my work has always demanded: decisions about a future nobody can see.

115
00:12:23,509 --> 00:12:31,160
In twenty years of network engineering and digital transformation, I have learned that a platform chosen today may still shape a business in ten years.

116
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:37,604
Building the three worlds of 2041 for this assignment showed me a different way to approach that kind of decision.

117
00:12:37,884 --> 00:12:45,686
Drawing the possibility tree forced me to give each branch a probability, and the less likely branches raised questions a single forecast would never have asked.

118
00:12:45,966 --> 00:12:55,208
Working backwards from a 2041 press release, the way Amazon writes the press release before the product exists, made the first steps for 2026 obvious.

119
00:12:55,488 --> 00:13:03,487
Counterfactual thinking kept the exercise honest: writing Overheat next to Symbiosis stopped me from falling in love with the optimistic future.

120
00:13:03,767 --> 00:13:09,955
What resonated most is that creativity here is not a talent but a structured skill, wild ideas plus strategy.

121
00:13:11,855 --> 00:13:13,399
How I will put it into practice.

122
00:13:13,679 --> 00:13:14,840
My plan is concrete.

123
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:25,163
From November, every new technical proposal I write will open with a one-page working backwards brief describing the result for the customer in three years, the assumptions behind it and the first test.

124
00:13:25,443 --> 00:13:38,725
Once a quarter, starting in January 2027, I will build a possibility tree for one roadmap decision, with three branches, nine sub-branches and probabilities, and review it three months later to see which branch reality followed.

125
00:13:39,005 --> 00:13:45,948
Before each major design review I will run one upward and one downward counterfactual with the team and record the risks it surfaces.

126
00:13:46,228 --> 00:13:57,211
Personally, I live between Riyadh and Stockholm, and I will turn those flights into fuzzy focus time: no screens, just my stimuli queue and a commonplace book, so slow hunches have somewhere to land.

127
00:13:57,491 --> 00:14:08,416
I will measure success simply: by June 2027, I will have built two possibility trees, completed the first three-month review, and recorded whether the findings changed a roadmap decision.

128
00:14:10,316 --> 00:14:15,006
All my sources and image credits are listed here, so every fact in this briefing can be checked.

129
00:14:16,906 --> 00:14:18,822
Finally, my AI declaration.

130
00:14:19,102 --> 00:14:30,189
I acknowledge the use of Claude, Canva AI and Superdesign to research and verify sources, structure and draft sections, generate the mood board and app visuals, and arrange and format the document.

131
00:14:30,469 --> 00:14:32,629
The output was used to support my own work.

132
00:14:32,909 --> 00:14:33,837
Thank you for watching.

